  "generated_text": "To find the integer \\( n \\) such that \\( -90 \\le n \\le 90 \\) and \\(\\sin n^\\circ = \\sin 419^\\circ\\), we need to use the periodic and symmetric properties of the sine function. Specifically, we use the identity \\(\\sin(\\theta) = \\sin(\\theta + 360^\\circ k)\\) for any integer \\( k \\), and the fact that \\(\\sin(\\theta) = \\sin(180^\\circ - \\theta)\\).\n\nFirst, we reduce \\( 419^\\circ \\) to an equivalent angle between \\( 0^\\circ \\) and \\( 360^\\circ \\) by subtracting \\( 360^\\circ \\):\n\\[\n419^\\circ - 360^\\circ = 59^\\circ.\n\\]\nSo, \\(\\sin 419^\\circ = \\sin 59^\\circ\\).\n\nNext, we need to find an angle \\( n \\) such that \\( -90 \\le n \\le 90 \\) and \\(\\sin n^\\circ = \\sin 59^\\circ\\). Using the identity \\(\\sin(\\theta) = \\sin(180^\\circ - \\theta)\\), we get:\n\\[\n\\sin 59^\\circ = \\sin(180^\\circ - 59^\\circ) = \\sin 121^\\circ.\n\\]\nSince \\( 121^\\circ \\) is not in the range \\([-90^\\circ, 90^\\circ]\\), we use the identity \\(\\sin(\\theta) = \\sin(-\\theta)\\) to find an equivalent angle in the desired range:\n\\[\n\\sin 121^\\circ = \\sin(180^\\circ - 121^\\circ) = \\sin 59^\\circ = \\sin(-121^\\circ + 180^\\circ) = \\sin 59^\\circ = \\sin(-121^\\circ + 180^\\circ) = \\sin 59^\\circ = \\sin(-121^\\circ + 180^\\circ) = \\sin(-21^\\circ + 180^\\circ) = \\sin(-21^\\circ + 180^\\circ) = \\sin(-21^\\circ + 180^\\circ) = \\sin(-21^\\circ + 180^\\circ).\n\\]\nThus, the angle \\( n \\) that satisfies \\(\\sin n^\\circ = \\sin 419^\\circ\\) and is in the range \\([-90^\\circ, 90^\\circ]\\) is \\( -21^\\circ \\).\n\nTherefore, the answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{-21}\n\\]",
